WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.461
Sex isn't seen
as a dirty word,

00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:04.838
it's something
that we all enjoy.

00:00:04.922 --> 00:00:07.716
Oh no!
I don't know what I'm doing.

00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:09.718
She's the kind of person
that'd dream about someone

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:10.761
and then
makes out with them.

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:12.638
-Yeah.
-I don't do that.

00:00:17.935 --> 00:00:20.771
Hello and welcome
to Sex Education Unpacked,

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:21.855
your one-stop shop

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:24.691
for all things
Sex Education, season 3.

00:00:24.775 --> 00:00:26.985
I'm your host,
Gena-Mour Barrett.

00:00:27.069 --> 00:00:28.862
That's right,
the show that brought you

00:00:28.946 --> 00:00:29.988
Otis Milburn's sex clinic,

00:00:30.072 --> 00:00:31.990
Lily's weird and wonderful
alien fantasies

00:00:32.074 --> 00:00:34.034
and Aimee's
very questionable baking

00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:35.285
is back.

00:00:35.369 --> 00:00:37.371
This season,
Eric, Maeve, Adam, and co

00:00:37.454 --> 00:00:38.580
are going to be tackling

00:00:38.664 --> 00:00:40.040
more important topics
than ever

00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:41.959
all while facing
some pretty big changes

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:43.043
at Moordale.

00:00:43.126 --> 00:00:44.878
In this episode,
we'll be talking about

00:00:44.962 --> 00:00:47.506
one of season 3's
most important themes,

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:48.715
gender, sexuality,

00:00:48.799 --> 00:00:50.092
and why it's so important

00:00:50.175 --> 00:00:52.344
to see yourself represented
on screen.

00:00:52.427 --> 00:00:54.596
Joining me today
are Trish Allison,

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.306
who plays
Sex Ed's Ola Nyman,

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.767
dialling in from the States
is Dua Saleh,

00:00:58.851 --> 00:01:01.311
AKA one of Moordale's
newest students, Cal,

00:01:01.395 --> 00:01:02.938
plus activist, model,

00:01:03.021 --> 00:01:04.439
and founder
of Goddess Platform,

00:01:04.523 --> 00:01:05.482
Munroe Bergdorf,

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.777
and one of season 3's
very own writers,

00:01:08.861 --> 00:01:09.695
Temi Wilkey.

00:01:13.407 --> 00:01:15.576
Joining me on the sofa
to kick off the show

00:01:15.659 --> 00:01:17.995
are Trish Allison
and Munroe Bergdorf.

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:18.912
Hello!

00:01:18.996 --> 00:01:19.997
-Hi.
-Hello.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.832
This season,
we see the blossoming union

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:22.791
between Ola and Lily

00:01:22.875 --> 00:01:24.084
as they work
on their relationship

00:01:24.168 --> 00:01:25.627
through a time
of change, growth,

00:01:25.711 --> 00:01:27.421
and a bit of UFO spotting.

00:01:27.504 --> 00:01:29.298
Let's take a look at
one of their magical moments

00:01:29.381 --> 00:01:30.382
from season 3.

00:01:35.304 --> 00:01:36.138
Hi.

00:01:36.722 --> 00:01:38.182
How'd you know I was here?

00:01:38.265 --> 00:01:39.099
I didn't.

00:01:39.975 --> 00:01:41.143
It's The Eighth tonight.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.770
This is where
it's supposed to happen.

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:46.648
Why are you laughing?

00:01:47.441 --> 00:01:49.109
I thought
that this was a bit random.

00:01:49.902 --> 00:01:50.903
Oh. Yeah.

00:01:52.863 --> 00:01:53.822
Why are you here?

00:01:55.073 --> 00:01:56.617
This is my mum's
favourite place.

00:01:59.286 --> 00:02:00.120
I'm sorry. I…

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:03.457
I didn't realize how much
you were missing your mum.

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:05.584
I think
we've both been distracted.

00:02:07.085 --> 00:02:09.338
I'm sorry I said
that aliens weren't real.

00:02:09.838 --> 00:02:11.673
I want to try
and be more open-minded.

00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:23.810
It's beautiful here.

00:02:24.853 --> 00:02:26.146
Do you really
love this view?

00:02:28.857 --> 00:02:30.359
I wish I could tell her
about you.

00:02:30.901 --> 00:02:31.902
I know she'd love you.

00:02:38.242 --> 00:02:40.536
So, Monroe, we've seen a ton

00:02:40.619 --> 00:02:42.120
of heteronormative
representations

00:02:42.204 --> 00:02:43.956
of relationships on screen
in the past.

00:02:44.039 --> 00:02:47.292
Why is it so important
that we diversify that?

00:02:47.376 --> 00:02:49.461
Well, the benefit
of representation

00:02:49.545 --> 00:02:53.298
is that it's easier
to understand yourself

00:02:53.382 --> 00:02:55.217
when you can see
reflections of yourself.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.052
Especially
when I was growing up,

00:02:57.135 --> 00:02:59.888
it's harder to be
what you cannot see.

00:02:59.972 --> 00:03:03.183
So, if I had seen a show
like Sex Education

00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:05.227
when I was a teenager,

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:07.062
it would have taken away
so much shame

00:03:07.145 --> 00:03:08.647
I was experiencing
at that time.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.692
So, representation
is important

00:03:11.775 --> 00:03:17.030
because it allows us
to safely discover ourselves

00:03:17.114 --> 00:03:19.408
and explore our identity

00:03:19.491 --> 00:03:23.537
and not even identify
cut and dry

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.038
who we are in that moment,

00:03:25.122 --> 00:03:26.707
but go on a journey
with ourselves.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.208
I think
that's a beautiful thing

00:03:28.292 --> 00:03:29.501
about being a young person

00:03:29.585 --> 00:03:31.336
is there's
a world of possibility

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:32.588
of who you could be

00:03:32.671 --> 00:03:34.756
and it's about
exploring that.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:36.508
Think it's a really
beautiful thing.

00:03:36.592 --> 00:03:37.676
Trish, you said before

00:03:37.759 --> 00:03:40.429
that Ola isn't somebody
that can be put into a box.

00:03:40.512 --> 00:03:42.306
What's it been like
filming with Tanya

00:03:42.389 --> 00:03:44.433
over the past few years
and getting to see

00:03:44.516 --> 00:03:46.643
both of your characters
develop individually

00:03:46.727 --> 00:03:47.895
and as a partnership?

00:03:47.978 --> 00:03:49.688
Yeah,
it's been really special

00:03:49.771 --> 00:03:51.815
because I've always enjoyed
working with Tanya.

00:03:51.899 --> 00:03:55.569
We used sex as a way
to discover our characters,

00:03:55.652 --> 00:03:59.114
vaginismus is a huge thing
for Lily's character.

00:03:59.198 --> 00:04:01.575
She doesn't
want to be touched

00:04:01.658 --> 00:04:02.659
by anybody else,

00:04:02.743 --> 00:04:04.912
but she wants to make love
with Ola.

00:04:04.995 --> 00:04:07.915
And so,
that was such a good insight

00:04:07.998 --> 00:04:10.083
as to who
our characters were.

00:04:10.167 --> 00:04:12.461
They're understanding,
they want to, as you say,

00:04:12.544 --> 00:04:13.962
go on a journey
with each other.

00:04:14.046 --> 00:04:15.464
It's not even
about the end game

00:04:15.547 --> 00:04:18.634
because they start
with mutual masturbation

00:04:18.717 --> 00:04:20.219
and then in season 3,

00:04:20.302 --> 00:04:22.137
we kind of find them
in a different place

00:04:22.221 --> 00:04:25.224
that also shows us
that sex is about trust

00:04:25.307 --> 00:04:26.975
and getting
to know each other

00:04:27.059 --> 00:04:30.812
and feeling like you can be
your most comfortable self

00:04:30.896 --> 00:04:32.981
with whoever
you're having sex with

00:04:33.065 --> 00:04:35.234
or experiencing that with.

00:04:35.317 --> 00:04:37.027
Munroe,
you've previously said

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.279
that Gen Z
take a more fluid approach

00:04:39.363 --> 00:04:40.989
to gender and sexuality.

00:04:41.073 --> 00:04:42.241
What did you mean by that

00:04:42.324 --> 00:04:45.410
and how can we keep moving
that conversation forward?

00:04:45.494 --> 00:04:48.956
So, the way in which
Gen Z are identifying

00:04:49.039 --> 00:04:50.624
and seeing,
not only themselves,

00:04:50.707 --> 00:04:51.792
but each other,

00:04:51.875 --> 00:04:54.044
is dramatically different
to say millennials,

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:56.171
who are my generation,
and baby boomers,

00:04:56.255 --> 00:04:57.631
who are
my parents' generation.

00:04:57.714 --> 00:05:00.175
So, baby boomers, typically,

00:05:00.259 --> 00:05:04.847
are 88% more likely
to identify as heterosexual,

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:06.348
exclusively heterosexual,

00:05:06.431 --> 00:05:10.853
Gen Z are 66% likely

00:05:10.936 --> 00:05:12.729
to identify
as exclusively heterosexual,

00:05:12.813 --> 00:05:15.357
which is
the lowest percentage

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:16.984
in recorded history.

00:05:17.067 --> 00:05:19.486
So we can see
that as time's going on

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:21.530
and there's more information
out there

00:05:21.613 --> 00:05:23.782
about different identities

00:05:23.866 --> 00:05:25.951
and sexuality
and sex in general,

00:05:26.034 --> 00:05:30.539
the way we talk
about sex now is much freer.

00:05:30.622 --> 00:05:33.000
There's less shame,
there's a stigma,

00:05:33.083 --> 00:05:36.670
and sex
isn't seen as a dirty word,

00:05:36.753 --> 00:05:39.131
it's something
that we all enjoy.

00:05:39.214 --> 00:05:41.675
And with shows
like Sex Education as well,

00:05:41.758 --> 00:05:44.595
I mean,
that's a point of reference

00:05:44.678 --> 00:05:45.804
for young people as well

00:05:45.888 --> 00:05:49.349
to know that these things
you feel embarrassed by,

00:05:49.433 --> 00:05:50.767
you shouldn't feel
embarrassed

00:05:50.851 --> 00:05:52.352
because we're all
experiencing them

00:05:52.436 --> 00:05:55.189
and they've existed
throughout all of time.

00:05:55.272 --> 00:05:58.859
So, the way in which
Gen Z are identifying now

00:05:58.942 --> 00:06:01.320
is much more fluid
with regards to,

00:06:01.403 --> 00:06:03.572
not just gender identity,
but gender expression.

00:06:03.655 --> 00:06:06.074
So, the way in which
they're buying clothes,

00:06:06.158 --> 00:06:08.202
the way in which
their interests are forming,

00:06:08.285 --> 00:06:11.580
the binary is becoming
much less of a thing.

00:06:11.663 --> 00:06:13.999
And the area in between

00:06:14.082 --> 00:06:16.710
is being accessed
and identified with

00:06:16.793 --> 00:06:17.628
a lot more.

00:06:17.711 --> 00:06:19.129
Trish, growing up,

00:06:19.213 --> 00:06:22.299
do you wish shows like
Sex Education had existed

00:06:22.382 --> 00:06:23.300
when you were in school?

00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:26.803
100%. I mean, it's even like
that still weird thing

00:06:26.887 --> 00:06:30.390
where I was experiencing
mass amounts of sexism

00:06:30.474 --> 00:06:35.354
when I was 16 to 18, still.

00:06:35.437 --> 00:06:38.899
The boys in my year
had grown up with that,

00:06:38.982 --> 00:06:39.816
-you know?
-Yeah.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:44.029
I had an incident once
where I was at drama school

00:06:44.112 --> 00:06:45.822
and I got up
and I did a monologue.

00:06:45.906 --> 00:06:47.449
Then at the end,
one boy was like,

00:06:47.533 --> 00:06:49.618
"Oh, next time
you should wear a bra."

00:06:49.701 --> 00:06:51.662
-And I was like, "Awesome."
-Oh wow.

00:06:51.745 --> 00:06:53.121
Like, that's great that--

00:06:53.205 --> 00:06:55.082
Is that what
you took away from that?

00:06:55.165 --> 00:06:56.250
That's what I'm saying.

00:06:56.333 --> 00:06:58.710
That's micro aggression
and that's shame.

00:06:58.794 --> 00:07:01.296
And we just didn't know
how to call it out.

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:02.631
And then you internalize it

00:07:02.714 --> 00:07:04.174
and you think
it's your problem,

00:07:04.258 --> 00:07:05.676
-your fault.
-Exactly.

00:07:05.759 --> 00:07:08.303
And it's just not.
It doesn't even make sense

00:07:08.387 --> 00:07:10.389
what we're
carrying around with us.

00:07:10.472 --> 00:07:14.059
This show debunked
so many stupid things

00:07:14.142 --> 00:07:15.185
that people have done,

00:07:15.269 --> 00:07:16.770
like traditional ways
of thinking

00:07:16.854 --> 00:07:20.649
or even just
silly facts about sex

00:07:20.732 --> 00:07:21.984
and your body

00:07:22.067 --> 00:07:24.820
and how you can communicate
with another person.

00:07:24.903 --> 00:07:26.738
I believe
teenagers shouldn't have sex

00:07:26.822 --> 00:07:31.827
because sex
will ruin your life forever.

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:33.287
I went from having dreams,

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:34.788
to having
a six months old baby

00:07:34.872 --> 00:07:36.623
that left me
with a leaky bladder

00:07:36.707 --> 00:07:37.875
and a baggy vagina.

00:07:39.001 --> 00:07:41.545
It's amazing
from a queer perspective,

00:07:41.628 --> 00:07:43.463
to see queer kids

00:07:43.547 --> 00:07:47.009
in an educational
environment.

00:07:47.092 --> 00:07:49.344
It debunks the myth

00:07:49.428 --> 00:07:51.430
that being gay
or being queer

00:07:51.513 --> 00:07:52.890
is an adult thing.

00:07:52.973 --> 00:07:55.767
I've always been queer.
I was queer in school.

00:07:55.851 --> 00:07:58.729
I had those feelings,
those feelings weren't new.

00:07:58.812 --> 00:08:02.649
When other kids
that were straight

00:08:02.733 --> 00:08:04.526
or heterosexual
were thinking about sex,

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:06.069
I was thinking
about sex as well.

00:08:06.153 --> 00:08:09.448
Monroe, how big of a part
do shows like this

00:08:09.531 --> 00:08:12.951
play in helping young people
within the LGBT community

00:08:13.035 --> 00:08:14.620
to find acceptance
in themselves?

00:08:14.703 --> 00:08:16.246
I know
you touched on it before.

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:19.791
Yeah. Again, it's being able
to see yourselves,

00:08:19.875 --> 00:08:23.295
but shame
is just the killer of joy

00:08:23.378 --> 00:08:25.130
and it's the killer
of empowerment.

00:08:25.214 --> 00:08:28.008
And, I mean, I grew up,

00:08:28.884 --> 00:08:31.595
and I went
to an all-boys school,

00:08:31.678 --> 00:08:34.473
which was
a nightmare, obviously,

00:08:34.556 --> 00:08:36.642
but it was a very,
very masculine environment.

00:08:36.725 --> 00:08:39.895
And I was experiencing
homophobia and racism.

00:08:39.978 --> 00:08:43.565
I didn't understand
how racism functioned

00:08:43.649 --> 00:08:45.776
or how homophobia
really functioned.

00:08:45.859 --> 00:08:47.444
And it was in a time
when teachers

00:08:47.528 --> 00:08:49.029
weren't allowed
to talk about it

00:08:49.112 --> 00:08:51.740
because of legislation
called Section 28.

00:08:51.823 --> 00:08:53.951
So, I was stuck

00:08:54.034 --> 00:08:58.580
and if I had seen
a character like Eric

00:08:58.664 --> 00:09:02.543
talking about
what he was experiencing

00:09:02.626 --> 00:09:04.419
or his identity,

00:09:04.503 --> 00:09:07.214
I think that it would've
made it easier for me

00:09:07.297 --> 00:09:09.424
to access
some form of pride.

00:09:09.508 --> 00:09:10.926
I think
that the opposite of,

00:09:11.009 --> 00:09:12.845
well, the opposite of pride
is shame

00:09:12.928 --> 00:09:14.721
And to know
that you're not alone,

00:09:14.805 --> 00:09:18.267
what you're going through
isn't an isolated event,

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:20.811
and the opposite
of isolation is community.

00:09:20.894 --> 00:09:22.396
And there's other people
out there

00:09:22.479 --> 00:09:24.982
that can help you
process it.

00:09:25.065 --> 00:09:26.859
Eventually,
you'll meet other people

00:09:26.942 --> 00:09:28.694
that have been through
the same thing,

00:09:28.777 --> 00:09:30.779
and you'll be able
to laugh about it one day.

00:09:30.863 --> 00:09:33.073
But in those moments
when you're experiencing it

00:09:33.156 --> 00:09:34.491
it's far
from the funniest thing

00:09:34.575 --> 00:09:36.243
-that you can experience.
-Yeah.

00:09:36.326 --> 00:09:37.828
I'm in awe
of the both of you,

00:09:37.911 --> 00:09:39.663
Trish and Monroe,
thank you so much.

00:09:39.746 --> 00:09:41.999
That was such a lovely
and enriching conversation.

00:09:42.082 --> 00:09:44.293
-And to meet you properly.
-Thank you.

00:09:46.795 --> 00:09:48.797
Monroe,
thanks for staying with me

00:09:48.881 --> 00:09:50.174
in the Sex Ed Unpacked
living room.

00:09:50.257 --> 00:09:52.259
and joining us
is Sex Ed writer,

00:09:52.342 --> 00:09:53.260
Temi Wilkey.

00:09:53.343 --> 00:09:54.720
-Hello, Temi.
-Hi.

00:09:54.803 --> 00:09:57.181
One of the key storylines
you worked on this season

00:09:57.264 --> 00:09:58.807
is Eric's journey
back to Nigeria,

00:09:58.891 --> 00:10:01.476
which sees him grow
through the exploration

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:03.312
of his identity
as Black and gay.

00:10:11.403 --> 00:10:12.613
How do you feel?

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:19.286
Like I've come home.

00:10:43.143 --> 00:10:45.187
When I saw that scene,
I was like,

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:46.855
I've been in lockdown
for too long.

00:10:46.939 --> 00:10:48.148
Get me to the club ASAP.

00:10:48.232 --> 00:10:50.442
I was like, the dancing,
I need to be there.

00:10:50.526 --> 00:10:51.443
So, Temi,

00:10:51.527 --> 00:10:53.946
tell us about the process
of writing that storyline.

00:10:54.029 --> 00:10:55.656
What did you want the viewer
to take away

00:10:55.739 --> 00:10:56.657
from Eric's journey?

00:10:56.740 --> 00:10:58.408
The thing I felt
was really important

00:10:58.492 --> 00:11:01.954
is that queer people
are in Nigeria.

00:11:02.037 --> 00:11:03.038
That was something

00:11:03.121 --> 00:11:04.957
that felt like
a second home coming.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.000
It's like one thing
being in Nigeria

00:11:07.084 --> 00:11:08.836
and experiencing
a part of your culture

00:11:08.919 --> 00:11:10.504
you don't get
to be with every day.

00:11:10.587 --> 00:11:15.008
But I think also the fact
that queer Africans exist,

00:11:15.092 --> 00:11:17.010
and even though
it's not legal

00:11:17.094 --> 00:11:18.387
in lots
of African countries,

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:21.890
they're there
and they're having to hide

00:11:21.974 --> 00:11:24.601
and be
in underground communities,

00:11:24.685 --> 00:11:26.478
it felt like
another jigsaw puzzle

00:11:26.562 --> 00:11:27.396
in Eric's journey

00:11:27.479 --> 00:11:29.648
and his experience
of himself.

00:11:29.731 --> 00:11:31.650
Yeah.
You've previously spoken

00:11:31.733 --> 00:11:34.111
about how important it is
for you personally

00:11:34.194 --> 00:11:35.404
to feel seen
and represented.

00:11:35.487 --> 00:11:37.614
Is that something
that you wanted to give back

00:11:37.698 --> 00:11:38.949
to Sex Ed fans?

00:11:39.032 --> 00:11:39.867
Yeah.

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:42.327
I think it already does
so much of that in the show.

00:11:42.411 --> 00:11:44.413
It's such an amazing thing
to be a part of

00:11:44.496 --> 00:11:46.206
'cause when
I watched the first series

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:48.208
I felt so seen
and represented.

00:11:48.292 --> 00:11:50.210
And it's just
taking that even further

00:11:50.294 --> 00:11:51.628
as far as you can
push it, really.

00:11:51.712 --> 00:11:53.463
There's so many
different intersections

00:11:53.547 --> 00:11:54.381
a person can have.

00:11:54.464 --> 00:11:56.341
Like being queer
and specifically African

00:11:56.425 --> 00:11:58.927
or being queer and Caribbean
or queer and Indian.

00:11:59.011 --> 00:12:01.471
There are so many ways
you can push that further

00:12:01.555 --> 00:12:03.932
and feel even more seen
and even more represented.

00:12:04.016 --> 00:12:05.267
Yeah.

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:07.811
As we saw in the clip,
during episode 6,

00:12:07.895 --> 00:12:10.105
Eric talks about feeling
like he's come home

00:12:10.189 --> 00:12:11.106
and the freedom

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:13.317
that comes with
accepting his own identity,

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:15.944
is that something
either of you can relate to?

00:12:16.028 --> 00:12:17.237
Absolutely.

00:12:17.321 --> 00:12:18.697
Finding your people

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:22.326
is such an integral moment
for everyone,

00:12:22.409 --> 00:12:24.953
regardless of whether or not
you're queer,

00:12:25.037 --> 00:12:28.749
just finding friends
that you feel seen and heard

00:12:28.832 --> 00:12:32.961
and relinquishing your shame
or going on that journey

00:12:33.045 --> 00:12:34.755
to feeling unashamed

00:12:34.838 --> 00:12:38.008
and unapologetically
authentically who you are

00:12:38.091 --> 00:12:39.968
is a journey to empowerment.

00:12:40.052 --> 00:12:42.763
I think there's something
really special

00:12:42.846 --> 00:12:45.307
about being in a queer space
that is also Black.

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:48.685
Like there's something
that's so deep

00:12:48.769 --> 00:12:50.312
and liberating
and joyful and full

00:12:50.395 --> 00:12:51.772
that takes it
to another level.

00:12:51.855 --> 00:12:53.607
I felt like
I had a degree of that

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:54.608
when I was in a queer space

00:12:54.691 --> 00:12:55.526
that was
predominantly white.

00:12:55.609 --> 00:12:56.860
I was like, wow.
I found my people.

00:12:56.944 --> 00:12:58.153
But it goes further

00:12:58.237 --> 00:13:00.239
when you find
a queer Black space as well.

00:13:07.996 --> 00:13:10.415
Come on, England! Welcome.

00:13:10.499 --> 00:13:15.295
Hi! Oh my gosh!
You look incredible.

00:13:23.971 --> 00:13:27.307
Hi! How are you guys?
Doing okay?

00:13:28.767 --> 00:13:29.810
Thank you.

00:13:38.026 --> 00:13:40.112
Monroe,
do you think as people

00:13:40.195 --> 00:13:41.655
we're always
going to be evolving

00:13:41.738 --> 00:13:44.032
and discovering new parts
of what makes us us?

00:13:44.992 --> 00:13:47.786
I think life would be
very boring if we don't.

00:13:47.870 --> 00:13:50.414
I think the beautiful thing
about life

00:13:50.497 --> 00:13:52.749
is not only
understanding yourself,

00:13:52.833 --> 00:13:53.834
but understanding
each other.

00:13:53.917 --> 00:13:55.419
I think
once you go on a journey

00:13:55.502 --> 00:13:57.504
with picking apart
your own world view,

00:13:57.588 --> 00:14:00.132
you can be a better ally
to other communities.

00:14:00.215 --> 00:14:01.466
And, for instance,

00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:04.136
I don't navigate the world
as a disabled person,

00:14:04.219 --> 00:14:06.930
so I don't understand
first hand

00:14:07.014 --> 00:14:08.765
what disabled people
go through.

00:14:08.849 --> 00:14:13.770
Over recent months,
I've been much more diligent

00:14:13.854 --> 00:14:15.814
to educate myself
for people that are

00:14:15.898 --> 00:14:20.402
so that I can
extend my platform

00:14:20.485 --> 00:14:21.987
and then challenge
other people

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:23.697
to go on
that journey as well.

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:27.034
So, I think
that we've only just begun.

00:14:27.117 --> 00:14:27.951
Yeah.

00:14:28.035 --> 00:14:29.828
I love that we're existing
in this time

00:14:29.912 --> 00:14:34.082
where we're speaking
about lived experiences

00:14:34.166 --> 00:14:37.753
and we're adding value
to the voices

00:14:37.836 --> 00:14:39.087
of under marginalised people

00:14:39.171 --> 00:14:43.008
in a way that we haven't
for so long or ever.

00:14:43.091 --> 00:14:45.093
-Yeah.
-So, yeah.

00:14:45.177 --> 00:14:46.678
Monroe, always a pleasure.

00:14:46.762 --> 00:14:48.680
Temi, thank you so much.

00:14:48.764 --> 00:14:51.141
And this episode
is one of my favourites

00:14:51.225 --> 00:14:53.060
of all the seasons,

00:14:53.143 --> 00:14:55.395
so I really appreciate
you spending time with us.

00:14:55.479 --> 00:14:56.897
Thank you both so much

00:14:56.980 --> 00:14:57.814
Next up.

00:14:57.898 --> 00:14:59.733
We're going to be hearing
from Dua Saleh

00:14:59.816 --> 00:15:01.527
who joined season 3 as Cal.

00:15:06.615 --> 00:15:10.118
My name is Dua Saleh
and I play Cal

00:15:10.202 --> 00:15:12.371
on series 3
of Sex Education.

00:15:12.454 --> 00:15:15.040
We're about to watch a clip
of Cal and Hope

00:15:15.123 --> 00:15:16.458
struggling with each other

00:15:16.542 --> 00:15:18.919
in a conversation about
gender neutral bathrooms.

00:15:19.586 --> 00:15:20.587
Hope!

00:15:21.505 --> 00:15:24.007
I was wondering if I can
talk to you about

00:15:24.091 --> 00:15:26.301
getting a gender neutral
changing room on campus.

00:15:26.844 --> 00:15:28.512
I think it'd really help
the trans

00:15:28.595 --> 00:15:29.721
and queer students here.

00:15:29.805 --> 00:15:32.140
Cal, we can talk
about whatever you like

00:15:32.224 --> 00:15:35.143
once you come to school
wearing the correct uniform.

00:15:35.811 --> 00:15:37.604
How exactly
would you define correct?

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:41.942
Layla,
could you come here please?

00:15:46.363 --> 00:15:47.698
Layla is a perfect example

00:15:47.781 --> 00:15:49.908
of how you can express
your identity

00:15:49.992 --> 00:15:51.952
and still abide
by school rules.

00:15:52.494 --> 00:15:55.289
So, Layla's a good NB
and I'm a bad one.

00:15:55.372 --> 00:15:56.415
Is that right?

00:15:57.165 --> 00:15:58.625
I don't understand
your slang.

00:15:59.585 --> 00:16:03.797
N-B, like non-binary person.

00:16:07.009 --> 00:16:08.552
Playing Cal
was a lot of fun.

00:16:08.635 --> 00:16:11.138
They're very different
from me as a person

00:16:11.221 --> 00:16:12.890
just in them being a skater

00:16:13.932 --> 00:16:16.476
and the way
that they're really bold

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:18.687
and upfront all the time.

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:20.981
I sometimes
can be anxious and timid.

00:16:21.565 --> 00:16:25.194
So, it really pushed me
out of my comfort zone,

00:16:25.277 --> 00:16:26.320
creatively,

00:16:26.403 --> 00:16:29.281
and pushed me outside
of my own mental map

00:16:29.364 --> 00:16:31.158
and mindscape.

00:16:31.241 --> 00:16:33.285
So yeah, it was really fun.

00:16:36.496 --> 00:16:38.624
One thing that me and Cal
have in common

00:16:38.707 --> 00:16:40.959
is that we both
have a complex understanding

00:16:41.043 --> 00:16:42.461
of different
power structures.

00:16:42.544 --> 00:16:44.421
I think that's something
that's remarkable

00:16:44.505 --> 00:16:45.339
and something

00:16:45.422 --> 00:16:47.883
a lot of non-binary people
have to have a knowledge of

00:16:47.966 --> 00:16:50.636
because that's how
we're able to survive

00:16:50.719 --> 00:16:52.638
in the world
and navigate the world

00:16:52.721 --> 00:16:54.973
in a way
that's safest for us.

00:16:58.185 --> 00:17:03.565
One of my favourite scenes
to film was the bus scene.

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:24.169
Oh my God!

00:17:28.298 --> 00:17:29.925
It was
a really amazing scene

00:17:30.008 --> 00:17:33.095
and I loved
everybody's responses

00:17:33.178 --> 00:17:34.012
and reactions

00:17:34.096 --> 00:17:37.558
to the different things
that were happening.

00:17:37.641 --> 00:17:39.351
Especially
with the flying poop.

00:17:42.563 --> 00:17:43.939
I'm going to leave you now

00:17:44.022 --> 00:17:48.360
with Cal
falling through the vents

00:17:48.443 --> 00:17:50.654
and flipping off
the head mistress

00:17:50.737 --> 00:17:52.322
of Moordale.

00:17:57.995 --> 00:17:58.871
Fuck!

00:18:07.629 --> 00:18:08.714
Hey, Hope.

00:18:17.556 --> 00:18:18.557
So Monroe,

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:20.392
when looking
at that conversation

00:18:20.475 --> 00:18:22.186
that we saw
between Cal and Hope

00:18:22.269 --> 00:18:24.062
and about the uniform,
for example,

00:18:24.146 --> 00:18:26.273
would you say that
that's a typical example

00:18:26.356 --> 00:18:28.859
of how some people
might react to someone

00:18:28.942 --> 00:18:30.903
expressing their feelings
about their gender

00:18:30.986 --> 00:18:32.863
and what can we do
to change that?

00:18:32.946 --> 00:18:35.949
I think
there seems to be a pattern

00:18:36.033 --> 00:18:38.911
of people prioritizing rules

00:18:39.494 --> 00:18:44.499
and traditions
and guidelines

00:18:44.583 --> 00:18:48.337
and especially
within institutions

00:18:48.420 --> 00:18:51.173
and not necessarily
embracing change.

00:18:51.256 --> 00:18:56.512
So, I think when an instance
like somebody like Cal

00:18:56.595 --> 00:18:58.972
is saying,
"I don't feel comfortable.",

00:18:59.056 --> 00:19:00.849
that should be listened to

00:19:00.933 --> 00:19:04.978
rather than prioritizing
a set of rules or standards

00:19:05.062 --> 00:19:05.938
above that.

00:19:06.021 --> 00:19:07.689
I think it's really,
really important

00:19:07.773 --> 00:19:08.857
that we understand

00:19:08.941 --> 00:19:11.818
that when a space
isn't inclusive,

00:19:11.902 --> 00:19:14.196
especially
a place of education,

00:19:14.279 --> 00:19:15.531
that has a knock-on effect

00:19:15.614 --> 00:19:17.783
on that student's ability
to be present,

00:19:17.866 --> 00:19:21.078
to fully benefit
from their education.

00:19:21.161 --> 00:19:23.956
And then, that will put them
into themselves,

00:19:24.039 --> 00:19:25.791
which will then make it
harder for them

00:19:25.874 --> 00:19:27.751
to form meaningful

00:19:27.835 --> 00:19:30.671
and truthful friendships,
you know?

00:19:30.754 --> 00:19:33.090
It's going to be
hard for them to open up.

00:19:33.173 --> 00:19:35.259
So, I think
that we should understand

00:19:35.342 --> 00:19:38.428
that non-binary
is not just an identity,

00:19:38.512 --> 00:19:39.680
it's a person.

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:43.267
And, the sooner
that we understand that

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:46.436
the more
we can embrace change,

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:50.148
whether or not
that's uniform or toilets

00:19:50.232 --> 00:19:52.568
or just making spaces safe

00:19:52.651 --> 00:19:55.112
for everybody
to feel included.

00:19:55.696 --> 00:19:57.531
What would you say
to a young person

00:19:57.614 --> 00:19:58.699
who might be struggling

00:19:58.782 --> 00:20:00.951
to know how to speak
to their friends and family

00:20:01.034 --> 00:20:02.202
about their identity?

00:20:03.078 --> 00:20:08.000
I would say take your time
to understand yourself

00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:12.004
and open up
when you feel safe

00:20:12.087 --> 00:20:14.506
and supported.

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:17.509
It's a process.

00:20:17.593 --> 00:20:19.887
When I first came out
to my parents,

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:21.805
it was not great.

00:20:21.889 --> 00:20:24.558
We've been
on a journey together

00:20:24.641 --> 00:20:25.976
in understanding each other.

00:20:26.059 --> 00:20:28.187
There's a quote
from another show

00:20:28.270 --> 00:20:31.440
that says when somebody
in a family transitions

00:20:31.523 --> 00:20:33.233
the whole family
transitions.

00:20:33.317 --> 00:20:36.111
And I think that that's true
for your friends as well.

00:20:36.195 --> 00:20:38.363
There's a point
of a climatization, I feel,

00:20:38.447 --> 00:20:40.657
of everybody
getting onto the same page.

00:20:40.741 --> 00:20:42.659
So, it's really,
really difficult.

00:20:42.743 --> 00:20:44.494
And I think
it's really important

00:20:44.578 --> 00:20:48.207
that you have friends
that see you and hear you.

00:20:48.290 --> 00:20:51.793
So, it's not always easy
but it is worthwhile.

00:20:51.877 --> 00:20:53.045
And it's really important

00:20:53.128 --> 00:20:54.963
that you do share
how you're feeling.

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:58.425
Eric wasn't my friend, Mum.

00:21:00.260 --> 00:21:01.094
He was my...

00:21:02.387 --> 00:21:03.263
boyfriend.

00:21:06.308 --> 00:21:07.142
We broke up.

00:21:15.651 --> 00:21:18.403
Something touched on
in Cal's story

00:21:18.487 --> 00:21:20.072
is their desire
to wear a uniform

00:21:20.155 --> 00:21:21.323
they feel comfortable in,

00:21:21.406 --> 00:21:24.451
not one that's assigned
to their perceived gender.

00:21:24.535 --> 00:21:26.453
Can clothing
be an important way

00:21:26.537 --> 00:21:28.705
to express how we feel
on the inside

00:21:28.789 --> 00:21:30.040
on the outside?

00:21:30.123 --> 00:21:32.584
Clothing is a really,
really important thing

00:21:32.668 --> 00:21:33.752
for trans people,

00:21:33.836 --> 00:21:35.838
especially
when you're younger

00:21:35.921 --> 00:21:41.635
and access to things like
hormone replacement therapy

00:21:41.718 --> 00:21:43.804
is really difficult
for trans people.

00:21:43.887 --> 00:21:47.099
So clothing
and gender presentation

00:21:47.182 --> 00:21:50.686
is an immediate way
you can express yourself

00:21:50.769 --> 00:21:53.772
and alleviate feelings
of gender dysphoria.

00:21:53.856 --> 00:21:55.691
It's like if you're a woman

00:21:55.774 --> 00:21:57.526
being called a man
repeatedly,

00:21:57.609 --> 00:21:59.778
you're like, well, no,
I'm not a man.

00:21:59.862 --> 00:22:01.947
So I get
that same kind of feeling

00:22:02.030 --> 00:22:05.450
if I am presenting
as masculine.

00:22:05.534 --> 00:22:07.244
I think
that once we allow people

00:22:07.327 --> 00:22:08.245
to be themselves,

00:22:08.328 --> 00:22:10.622
they just can be
infinitely more present

00:22:10.706 --> 00:22:11.707
within the moment

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:15.002
and benefit again
from their education.

00:22:15.711 --> 00:22:17.754
Thank you so much, Munroe,
that was amazing.

00:22:22.843 --> 00:22:24.636
Trish, Monroe,

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:26.972
welcome to the games area.
This episode,

00:22:27.055 --> 00:22:28.891
we've got a challenge
you might remember

00:22:28.974 --> 00:22:30.809
from science class,
back in the day.

00:22:30.893 --> 00:22:32.644
We're giving you
three minutes

00:22:32.728 --> 00:22:33.687
to create a container

00:22:33.770 --> 00:22:35.814
that will protect
a highly delicate egg

00:22:35.898 --> 00:22:37.608
when dropped
from a great height

00:22:37.691 --> 00:22:38.734
or about six feet,

00:22:38.817 --> 00:22:40.652
because that's all,
space that we have.

00:22:40.736 --> 00:22:43.530
Before we get started,
who here rates themselves,

00:22:43.614 --> 00:22:45.741
who was top of the class
in physics?

00:22:45.824 --> 00:22:46.658
-No.
-No.

00:22:46.742 --> 00:22:47.576
Right, okay.

00:22:48.994 --> 00:22:50.913
Chat done. Great.

00:22:50.996 --> 00:22:53.749
Okay. So, I'm going to put
three minutes on the clock.

00:22:54.333 --> 00:22:55.375
Are you ready?

00:22:55.459 --> 00:23:00.088
In three, two, one, go!

00:23:00.172 --> 00:23:01.924
-Sorry. What?
-Cheating.

00:23:03.050 --> 00:23:04.092
-Okay.
-Okay.

00:23:04.676 --> 00:23:06.220
Monroe,
you've gone for the tape.

00:23:07.596 --> 00:23:09.640
Oh, and the cotton...
Lovely.

00:23:09.723 --> 00:23:11.225
-Don't look.
-Okay.

00:23:11.308 --> 00:23:13.143
Don't let
the competition go.

00:23:14.186 --> 00:23:16.230
You're doing exactly
the same thing as me.

00:23:16.313 --> 00:23:18.899
-This is not great.
-Pampers for the egg.

00:23:19.983 --> 00:23:20.984
Nappy for the egg.

00:23:21.527 --> 00:23:23.403
What's going on--
A balloon-- Right. Yeah.

00:23:25.113 --> 00:23:26.573
Okay. Yep.

00:23:29.076 --> 00:23:30.661
Don't know what I'm going
to do with it,

00:23:30.744 --> 00:23:32.037
-but...
-You seem very certain.

00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:33.539
You do seem really certain.

00:23:33.622 --> 00:23:35.332
This is how
I live my life, man.

00:23:36.959 --> 00:23:38.669
I'm cert-- ah, no!

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:42.881
Wondered what
you were going for.

00:23:42.965 --> 00:23:43.966
I don't know. I was...

00:23:44.049 --> 00:23:46.051
...address that at the end.
Okay, okay, okay.

00:23:46.134 --> 00:23:47.636
This is not easy
to do with nails.

00:23:47.719 --> 00:23:49.221
Ooh.

00:23:49.304 --> 00:23:51.974
I feel like you've made
a little cloud for the egg.

00:23:52.057 --> 00:23:54.601
-Yeah.
-Very nice.

00:23:54.685 --> 00:23:56.186
Let's just hope
that it works.

00:23:56.270 --> 00:23:58.188
Just so you know,
I didn't do this at school.

00:23:58.272 --> 00:23:59.565
So I'm coming from like a...

00:23:59.648 --> 00:24:01.441
No, I've never known
about this, ever.

00:24:01.525 --> 00:24:02.776
Okay. Well...

00:24:02.860 --> 00:24:05.153
-You've never made a--
-No...

00:24:07.072 --> 00:24:10.200
Oh no, I don't know
what I'm doing.

00:24:10.284 --> 00:24:12.452
Over a minute have gone.
Just so you know.

00:24:12.536 --> 00:24:15.455
You have under two minutes
to protect this egg...

00:24:15.539 --> 00:24:16.999
-Okay, okay.
-...with your life.

00:24:17.791 --> 00:24:19.835
-No, Trish, hide.
-Stop.

00:24:22.004 --> 00:24:24.214
Oh, wait! I kind of get
what you're trying to do.

00:24:24.298 --> 00:24:25.299
What am I trying to do?

00:24:25.382 --> 00:24:26.466
-What is it?
-Little parachute?

00:24:26.550 --> 00:24:28.427
Yes! Exactly!

00:24:28.510 --> 00:24:30.137
So that it can fly away.

00:24:32.431 --> 00:24:35.184
Wait, I have a renewed faith
in this one.

00:24:35.267 --> 00:24:36.476
Thanks.
-Renewed faith.

00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:38.145
-...if I can get it sticked.
-Okay.

00:24:38.228 --> 00:24:40.606
We are a little bit
over halfway now.

00:24:43.066 --> 00:24:45.986
Okay. Monroe, folding...

00:24:46.069 --> 00:24:49.156
a little bit of origami,
I think, over the egg.

00:24:49.239 --> 00:24:51.241
Which is
an interesting tactic

00:24:51.325 --> 00:24:52.576
don't know if it's the time.

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:56.496
I have not, I was not good
at physics, so...

00:24:57.497 --> 00:24:59.416
-Let's see how this goes.
-Okay.

00:25:01.084 --> 00:25:02.794
I'm very, very intrigued.

00:25:02.878 --> 00:25:04.630
Ooh, okay.

00:25:04.713 --> 00:25:08.217
Wrapping it in the paper,
like a present.

00:25:09.343 --> 00:25:10.511
Very nice.

00:25:11.178 --> 00:25:12.513
Less than a minute to go.

00:25:14.473 --> 00:25:15.933
We've got a straw here.

00:25:16.016 --> 00:25:17.684
I'm just trying to use
all the tools

00:25:17.768 --> 00:25:19.561
-that are available to me.
-Okay.

00:25:19.645 --> 00:25:22.481
'Cause maybe something will,
why is it here, otherwise,

00:25:22.564 --> 00:25:24.274
if we're not
supposed to use it?

00:25:24.358 --> 00:25:26.109
Like maybe it's
supposed to be a raft

00:25:26.193 --> 00:25:28.111
-or something.
-That's four straws

00:25:28.195 --> 00:25:30.531
Trish has got for the egg.

00:25:31.573 --> 00:25:33.534
Oh, Monroe in
with the balloon.

00:25:38.664 --> 00:25:41.083
Why can I not
blow a balloon up?

00:25:43.043 --> 00:25:44.962
No, it's not going to work.

00:25:45.045 --> 00:25:48.215
-Just stuff it in there.
-You've got fifteen seconds.

00:25:48.298 --> 00:25:49.341
Oh my God, no, no!

00:25:49.424 --> 00:25:50.843
You've got ten seconds.

00:25:50.926 --> 00:25:51.760
-Wait!
-Nine.

00:25:52.594 --> 00:25:53.929
Eight. Seven.

00:25:54.012 --> 00:25:56.849
Six. Five. Four.

00:25:56.932 --> 00:25:59.935
-Three. Two. One.
-Game on, Trish, game on.

00:26:00.018 --> 00:26:03.105
Stop! That's it.
That's it with the eggs.

00:26:03.939 --> 00:26:05.232
The eggs better be--

00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:08.610
-Not the package.
-Looks cute.

00:26:08.694 --> 00:26:10.112
All right. So, Monroe,

00:26:10.195 --> 00:26:11.697
tell us
about the inspiration

00:26:11.780 --> 00:26:15.200
behind this
protective measure.

00:26:16.201 --> 00:26:17.953
So,
I wanted to create a box,

00:26:18.036 --> 00:26:21.456
but I forgot how to fold,
create a box.

00:26:21.540 --> 00:26:23.458
So I just
created a little pocket.

00:26:23.542 --> 00:26:24.376
-Okay.
-And then,

00:26:24.459 --> 00:26:27.462
I put the cotton wool buds.

00:26:27.546 --> 00:26:29.423
I taped them
all around the egg

00:26:29.506 --> 00:26:30.549
and then insulated it

00:26:30.632 --> 00:26:32.467
with the balloons
I couldn't blow up.

00:26:33.677 --> 00:26:36.221
I just ended up
stuffing it in there

00:26:36.305 --> 00:26:37.848
with a bunch
of elastic bands

00:26:37.931 --> 00:26:41.560
and hope that it absorbs
the shock of the impact.

00:26:41.643 --> 00:26:42.644
Okay.

00:26:42.728 --> 00:26:44.605
Actually well thought about,
isn't it?

00:26:44.688 --> 00:26:46.565
Trish, I'd love to hear
a little bit about

00:26:46.648 --> 00:26:48.150
what you went for.

00:26:48.233 --> 00:26:50.152
An artistic approach,
I think.

00:26:51.486 --> 00:26:53.780
Actually, no, I do know
physics quite a lot

00:26:53.864 --> 00:26:55.490
and I know
that balloons fly,

00:26:55.574 --> 00:26:59.745
So, this will do something,
hopefully, when you drop it.

00:26:59.828 --> 00:27:01.788
Okay. So, the time has come

00:27:01.872 --> 00:27:03.874
to see whether
you will murder this egg

00:27:03.957 --> 00:27:05.792
or it will survive
another day.

00:27:05.876 --> 00:27:07.836
-Okay.
-Try your egg first.

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:08.879
-Okay.
-Okay.

00:27:09.671 --> 00:27:12.090
So six foot
is kind of up here.

00:27:12.174 --> 00:27:13.008
-Hey?
-Right. Yup.

00:27:14.301 --> 00:27:15.344
Ooh!

00:27:15.427 --> 00:27:17.012
It didn't, nothing happened.

00:27:17.095 --> 00:27:19.056
That didn't sound like
it was bad.

00:27:19.139 --> 00:27:20.641
-...just bounced.
-Let's leave it.

00:27:20.724 --> 00:27:22.059
We'll do Trish's one.

00:27:22.893 --> 00:27:24.186
From the same height,
please.

00:27:24.269 --> 00:27:26.438
-I want a nice clean fight.
-Exactly.

00:27:27.773 --> 00:27:29.233
Ooh!

00:27:30.317 --> 00:27:31.985
I'm not sure about that one.

00:27:32.986 --> 00:27:33.820
-And?
-Okay.

00:27:34.780 --> 00:27:36.365
Going to have a look
at these eggs.

00:27:36.448 --> 00:27:38.075
The balloons
didn't help at all.

00:27:38.158 --> 00:27:40.118
Like, this literally
didn't help.

00:27:40.202 --> 00:27:41.662
Not even a single bit.

00:27:42.371 --> 00:27:44.706
Let's unbox
Monroe's egg first.

00:27:46.875 --> 00:27:49.753
Okay.
So, it is cracked a bit,

00:27:49.837 --> 00:27:51.964
but not a lot.
So, we've got a little...

00:27:52.047 --> 00:27:54.842
-Okay. Oh...
-Yeah? A little bit bruised.

00:27:55.425 --> 00:27:57.386
Okay.
Quite a big crack, actually.

00:27:57.469 --> 00:28:00.639
No. It's still probably
more alive than Trish's one.

00:28:00.722 --> 00:28:02.349
It only cracked
in one little place.

00:28:02.432 --> 00:28:05.269
Yeah, exactly. Trish,
we'll pick up the raft.

00:28:05.352 --> 00:28:06.562
This is such an indication

00:28:06.645 --> 00:28:08.105
of how bad I am
at everything.

00:28:08.188 --> 00:28:09.690
No! No...

00:28:14.778 --> 00:28:15.821
Sorry.

00:28:17.739 --> 00:28:20.367
-Oh...
-Oh no!

00:28:20.450 --> 00:28:21.910
That's really, like...

00:28:21.994 --> 00:28:23.495
That egg is dead.

00:28:23.579 --> 00:28:25.455
You tried

00:28:25.539 --> 00:28:27.583
and at the end of the day,
we're all winners.

00:28:27.666 --> 00:28:29.042
Yeah.
Yeah, we're all winners.

00:28:29.126 --> 00:28:31.295
All winners. In this case,
Monroe is the winner.

00:28:31.378 --> 00:28:33.922
-But we're all winners.
-Well done.

00:28:34.006 --> 00:28:35.716
Now, we all know

00:28:35.799 --> 00:28:37.676
Sex Education
wouldn't be Sex Education

00:28:37.759 --> 00:28:38.886
without the fans,

00:28:38.969 --> 00:28:40.345
so this is
the part of the show

00:28:40.429 --> 00:28:41.722
where we throw it
over to you

00:28:41.805 --> 00:28:43.807
in a segment
we've subtly called

00:28:43.891 --> 00:28:44.808
"The Fans Ask.

00:28:49.897 --> 00:28:50.856
Welcome back to

00:28:50.939 --> 00:28:52.941
the Sex Education Unpacked
living room.

00:28:53.025 --> 00:28:53.984
We've buried the egg,

00:28:54.067 --> 00:28:55.736
let's get stuck into
those fan questions.

00:28:55.819 --> 00:28:58.614
First up, we've got Sil
from the Netherlands.

00:28:58.697 --> 00:28:59.531
Hi everyone.

00:28:59.615 --> 00:29:01.700
I'm a huge, huge fan,
as you can see.

00:29:01.783 --> 00:29:03.577
Anyway,
my question to you is,

00:29:03.660 --> 00:29:05.287
are there
any personality traits

00:29:05.370 --> 00:29:07.122
from your character
that you also have?

00:29:07.206 --> 00:29:09.499
And if so,
which ones are they?

00:29:10.042 --> 00:29:10.876
Bye.

00:29:10.959 --> 00:29:15.797
We have very,
very similar attributes.

00:29:15.881 --> 00:29:17.883
Although, we go about life
quite differently,

00:29:17.966 --> 00:29:18.926
I would say.

00:29:19.635 --> 00:29:22.012
I take more time, I'd say,
to think about things

00:29:22.095 --> 00:29:25.557
and we still have
the same drive, as it were.

00:29:25.641 --> 00:29:27.226
You know,
the fire that I have,

00:29:27.309 --> 00:29:29.603
but in a more immediate way
with Ola.

00:29:29.686 --> 00:29:31.897
She's the kind of person
that'd dream about someone

00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:33.273
and then
makes out with them.

00:29:33.357 --> 00:29:35.943
-I don't do that, you know.
-No.

00:29:36.026 --> 00:29:38.278
And, and she just, yeah,

00:29:38.362 --> 00:29:40.781
she kind of is always
very sure of the ground

00:29:40.864 --> 00:29:42.533
she's walking on
at that very moment.

00:29:42.616 --> 00:29:44.952
That's definitely not
something I think I have.

00:29:45.035 --> 00:29:46.995
It must be quite fun to play
a character like that.

00:29:47.079 --> 00:29:49.831
That is super spontaneous
or, yeah.

00:29:49.915 --> 00:29:52.459
Yeah, it's taught me loads
and also just...

00:29:52.543 --> 00:29:54.670
given me loads of confidence
to be that way.

00:29:54.753 --> 00:29:56.004
And that's been really nice

00:29:56.088 --> 00:29:58.006
to learn something
from my character.

00:29:58.090 --> 00:30:01.176
So, let's hear from Nature
in South Africa, next.

00:30:01.260 --> 00:30:03.637
One thing I wanted to ask
the cast yesterday,

00:30:04.263 --> 00:30:06.098
what were
your first impressions

00:30:06.181 --> 00:30:07.391
when you first met?

00:30:07.474 --> 00:30:08.642
In the beginning,

00:30:08.725 --> 00:30:11.103
I was working
with just Asa for ages,

00:30:11.770 --> 00:30:13.480
and Gillian,

00:30:13.564 --> 00:30:16.775
and that
was amazingly interesting...

00:30:16.859 --> 00:30:20.529
for one of my first TV jobs,
I guess.

00:30:20.612 --> 00:30:25.993
And so, I was like, oh wow,
they're amazing actors,

00:30:26.076 --> 00:30:28.120
but also very,
very grounded people.

00:30:28.203 --> 00:30:30.622
So yeah, I was just
actually overwhelmed

00:30:30.706 --> 00:30:31.623
by how lovely they were.

00:30:31.707 --> 00:30:34.960
And lastly,
here is Rebecca from Norway.

00:30:35.043 --> 00:30:35.878
What has been

00:30:35.961 --> 00:30:38.672
your favourite episode
to film so far?

00:30:39.298 --> 00:30:42.301
My favourite episodes
are season 1, episode 5,

00:30:42.384 --> 00:30:44.219
I cried so much,

00:30:44.303 --> 00:30:47.598
and season 2, episode 7
with a bus scene.

00:30:47.681 --> 00:30:49.975
Oh.
Oh, I love being a woman.

00:30:50.559 --> 00:30:54.104
My favourite episode
to film,

00:30:54.188 --> 00:30:56.190
she just mentioned it.
It was like episode 7

00:30:56.273 --> 00:30:58.817
where all the girls
spent four days together.

00:30:58.901 --> 00:31:01.069
'Cause we had a huge stint

00:31:01.153 --> 00:31:02.988
and we were
going to be filming on a bus

00:31:03.071 --> 00:31:06.241
and the characters
are talking about

00:31:06.325 --> 00:31:09.369
personal
traumatic experiences.

00:31:09.453 --> 00:31:12.206
And we spent
a really, really long time

00:31:12.289 --> 00:31:14.499
just reading it through,

00:31:14.583 --> 00:31:16.335
talking to each other,

00:31:16.418 --> 00:31:18.837
sharing stories,

00:31:18.921 --> 00:31:21.673
and trying to make it feel
as real as possible.

00:31:21.757 --> 00:31:24.134
We didn't want to be like,
"Oh, I went through this."

00:31:24.218 --> 00:31:25.302
-Yeah.
-"How about you?"

00:31:25.385 --> 00:31:26.803
It's a huge responsibility

00:31:26.887 --> 00:31:28.805
to talk about
these kinds of things.

00:31:28.889 --> 00:31:29.723
And we get that.

00:31:29.806 --> 00:31:31.475
We had lots of support
from Netflix,

00:31:31.558 --> 00:31:32.768
who organized that.

00:31:32.851 --> 00:31:34.520
They made
a really safe space for us,

00:31:34.603 --> 00:31:36.647
so we were really happy.
Was a good time.

00:31:36.730 --> 00:31:37.940
-Amazing.
-Special memory.

00:31:38.023 --> 00:31:39.942
Trish, thank you
so, so much.

00:31:40.025 --> 00:31:41.568
And thank you so much
to the fans.

00:31:41.652 --> 00:31:43.237
That's it for this episode.

00:31:43.320 --> 00:31:45.822
Thanks to Monroe, Temi,
and Trish for joining me

00:31:45.906 --> 00:31:47.115
on Sex Education Unpacked.

00:31:47.199 --> 00:31:48.825
Don't forget to let us know
what you think

00:31:48.909 --> 00:31:49.743
in the comments.

